From 6dc7bfa6f7179ce5f88f92629d28a1b4b1357466 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:41:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] filesystem/posix: a read of a played file that fails with EIO
 is tried again, bounded

A drive that the kernel resets fails the requests it had in flight with EIO. In issue #16 those
were readahead requests and the page cache read the pages again by itself, so read(2) got its
data; where the kernel's own read fails too (the reset still settling, a drive that comes back
slowly), read(2) gives EIO, CFileStreamBuffer takes any failed read for the end of the file
("Error reading file - assuming eof"), and the video ends although a read a moment later would
work.

CPosixFile::SetReadRetry(), which CDVDInputStreamFile asks for after it opens a file on POSIX,
makes CPosixFile::Read() try a read that failed with EIO again, at the same byte (the offset is
set back before each try, so a try never reads shifted bytes; a short read is a success as
before), after 0.1, 0.25, 0.5 and 1 s and then every 2 s, for at most 15 s after the first error
(READ_RETRY_WINDOW: half the kernel's 30 s SCSI command timeout, which a drive that does not answer
has already waited; a USB reset takes 0.2 s, a hard disk spins up again in some seconds). Not
tried again:

- a file that is not on a block device of this machine (its st_dev has no /sys/dev/block entry:
  network and FUSE file systems, tmpfs), said once when it is opened;
- an error other than EIO (ENXIO, ENODEV and the rest), said once;
- a drive that is gone: its block device left /sys, or the state of its SCSI device (or NVMe
  controller) is offline, transport-offline, deleted, cancel, dead or deleting, checked before
  each wait: an unplugged drive ends the video within a try, not after the window;
- after a give-up: the next reads of that file fail at once, as before.

The waits end within 20 ms when the player stops: CDVDInputStreamFile::Abort(), which
CVideoPlayer::CloseFile() calls from its own thread, sets a flag that the waits read (it touches
nothing else, as Close() may run meanwhile). A try that the drive does not answer waits in the
kernel as any read does: the worst case for stopping is the one of RC6, a read that the kernel
holds up to its command timeout. The time of the tries is given back to the deadline of the
demuxer by the commit before. kodi.log: "File: reading <file> failed at byte N (Input/output
error): the read is tried again ...", "File: <file> reads again at byte N, M ms after the first
error, at try K", or why it was given up. Every other reader of files (CFile without the player)
keeps the reads of RC6.

Host test (readretry_test.cpp: PosixFile.cpp and IFile.cpp compiled for the host, read(),
access() and open() wrapped by the linker): 14 cases, 87 checks, among them EIO on three reads
then success (same bytes, every try at the failing byte, 0.92 s), a short read followed by EIO,
EIO for good (given up within the window, the next read at once), the block device leaving /sys
before and during the tries, an offline SCSI device, a stop during a wait (the read ends 4 ms
after it), ENXIO, a file on tmpfs and a reader that did not ask. Against PosixFile.cpp of RC6,
30 checks fail. Not run with a drive that the kernel resets.
